       <tr><td><strong>Combined protection</strong></td><td>Evidence + implementation + legal accountability across the river system.</td><td>Current strategic hypothesis; sequencing remains under research.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="focus-head">
    <div><div class="kicker">Non-duplication</div><h3>Existing actors already hold different pieces of the river story</h3></div>
    <p>Converging Waters should connect functions where useful, not absorb or rebrand work that already has owners and history.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="legal-grid">
    <article class="legal-card"><h3>CEPROSI + Terre des Hommes</h3><p>Long-term education, ceremony, cleanup, youth guardianship and process memory behind the Canchis milestone.</p></article>
    <article class="legal-card"><h3>Riberas / ACIRIWI + Calca</h3><p>Riverbank recovery, environmental education, Corredor Verde and a verified municipal cooperation pathway in the middle corridor.</p></article>
    <article class="legal-card"><h3>Women-led networks + public authorities</h3><p>Regional advocacy and articulation around contamination, headwaters and the Canchis precedent; no support for CW is inferred.</p></article>
  </div>

  <div class="legal-boundary">
    <b>Current working thesis:</b>
    protect the river by making existing rights language operational where it already exists, building credible evidence and stewardship capacity, coordinating across jurisdictions and using additional legal routes only where they add concrete reach or enforceability. The complete Canchis operative text, implementation acts and any formal Willkamayu representative remain open research dependencies.
  </div>

